WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.747
This deck includes a portable SQL practice engine in the browser.

00:00:04.747 --> 00:00:06.872
It is not a Spark cluster.

00:00:06.872 --> 00:00:11.537
We use it to practice the relational logic that will transfer to Spark SQL, such as grouping, joins, windows,

00:00:11.537 --> 00:00:13.702
and grain checks.

00:00:13.702 --> 00:00:21.222
Spark-specific file reading and Delta operations should be run in Fabric or another Spark environment.

00:00:21.222 --> 00:00:24.882
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:24.882 --> 00:00:30.362
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:30.362 --> 00:00:33.942
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:33.942 --> 00:00:38.082
In a real setting such as a retail pipeline aggregating high-volume sales data across partitions,

00:00:38.082 --> 00:00:45.087
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:45.087 --> 00:00:46.867
before discussing syntax.

00:00:46.867 --> 00:00:53.360
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
